{"target": "minidb/functions.py, minidb/parser.py (reviewed from planner's side)", "findings": ["minidb/functions.py: SCALARS and AGGREGATES have two different calling conventions (scalars are `f(*args)`, aggregates are `f(list_of_values)`) but the published interface only says \"SCALARS, AGGREGATES dicts\", so engine can easily call one like the other and get a TypeError -> state the convention in the published notes: `SCALARS[name](*arg_values)` and `AGGREGATES[name](values_list)`.", "minidb/functions.py: AGGREGATES['COUNT'] is `_count`, which counts non-NULL entries, so there is no way to express COUNT(*), which must count rows including all-NULL ones -> either document that the engine must pass one non-NULL sentinel per row for COUNT(*) (e.g. `[True] * len(rows)`), or add an explicit `COUNT_STAR` entry; otherwise `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t` under-counts rows whose value is NULL.", "minidb/functions.py: `_value_class` puts bool in its own class, so `compare(True, 1)` and `ABS(True)` raise QueryError, and MIN/MAX over a column holding booleans and numbers fails -> if booleans are expected in table data, fold _BOOLEAN into _NUMBER, or document that bool is a distinct comparison class so the engine's ORDER BY error message matches.", "minidb/functions.py: compare() returns None for NULL, so a caller doing `if compare(a, b) < 0` gets a TypeError instead of unknown -> the engine must branch on `result is None` first; worth stating in the published notes, since spec requires unknown (not an error, not false-by-accident) for any comparison with NULL.", "minidb/functions.py: `_LIKE_CACHE` is an unbounded module-level dict keyed by raw pattern text, so a long-running process leaks one compiled regex per distinct pattern -> cap it (e.g. functools.lru_cache on the compile helper).", "minidb/parser.py: the parser hard-codes token kinds and spellings that tokens.py never published ('KEYWORD', 'IDENT', 'PUNCT', 'OP', 'NUMBER', '*' and '.' as OP/PUNCT respectively) -> get tokens.py to publish the exact kind set and which kind carries '*', '.', ',' and '(' ')', otherwise `SELECT *` and `t.*` silently fail to parse.", "minidb/parser.py: parse() does not wrap non-QueryError exceptions from tokenize() or from token attribute access, so anything unexpected escapes as AttributeError/TypeError from a module whose published contract is QueryError-only -> wrap the body of parse() in `try/except QueryError: raise/except Exception as exc: raise QueryError(...) from exc`.", "minidb/parser.py: `_optional_alias()` accepts any IDENT as an alias for both select items and table refs, so a mistyped keyword ('SELECT a FROM t WHER x') is swallowed as an alias and the query silently means something else -> only accept an alias after AS, or reject an unconsumed IDENT that is followed by another IDENT/expression.", "minidb/parser.py: `_select_item` computes source_text as `sql[start:next_token_pos]`, which includes the whitespace and any comment between the expression and the following token; SelectItem.output_name() only strips whitespace, so `SELECT price * 2 /*c*/ FROM t` is named 'price*2/*c*/' -> record the end position of the last token consumed by the expression instead of the start of the next token.", "minidb/parser.py: the listing is truncated inside `_and_expression`, so the parsing of NOT, comparisons, IS NULL / IS NOT NULL, IN (...), LIKE, unary minus and function calls could not be reviewed; those are exactly the productions the planner depends on for BinOp('IN', x, tuple) and UnaryOp('IS NULL', x) shapes -> re-send the remainder of the file for review."]}
